n18×500 = 9,000\n\n18×47 = 846\n\nAdding all together: 200,000 +18,800 = 218,800; 218,800 +9,000 =227,800; 227,800 +846=228,646. Same result. Perfect.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 228,646. I need to present this in the required format. The user had a template where they first broke down into ones, tens, hundreds steps, then summed. So in the answer, following the template:\n\nThe product of 418 and 547 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. 418 × 7 = 2,926\n\n2. 418 × 40 = 16,720\n\n3. 418 × 500 = 209,000\n\nSumming these: 2,926 +16,720 =19,646; then 19,646 +209,000 =228,646.\n\nFinal answer: 228,646. So the answer box should be \\boxed{228646}\n\nWait, the user's original question had the numbers 418 and 547, but in their example, the first question was 27 and 153. The user provided an example where the reasoning was split into three steps because the second number (153) has three digits. So following that structure, the answer should follow the same template.\n\nSo in the problem, the user wants the reasoning to mention each digit place (ones, tens, hundreds) for the three-digit number (547 in this case). So the reasoning steps should be:\n\n1. Multiply 418 by the ones digit (7): 2926\n\n2. Multiply 418 by the tens digit (40): 16,720\n\n3. Multiply 418 by the hundreds digit (500): 209,000\n\nThen sum them up. So the answer should be formatted exactly like the example given, with the sum steps. Alright, that's correct. So I need to present the reasoning as per the template. The initial mistake was when I misread the numbers but now corrected.\n</think>", '']
