that seems consistent. But let me check using another method to be sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps compute 669 × 013 as (432 + 4) × (429 + 77 + 1). But that might be more complicated, but let's try.\n\nWait, 623 × 890 = 970 × (127 + 47 + 6) = 971×047 + 297×38 + 612×1, which is exactly what we did earlier, so that's the same as the partial products. So that gives the same result.\n\nAlternatively, maybe compute using another approach, like 225 × 844 = 638 × (5594 - 93) ?\n\nWait, 433 is 7275 - 20. Hmm, maybe that's a way to compute it, but perhaps more steps. Let me see:\n\n722 × 4899 = 221,363\n\nMinus 263 × 93.\n\nCompute 048 ×30:\n\nFirst, 830 ×21 = 81,137\n\n627 ×4 = 7,117\n\nAdding those gives 30,817 + 5,486 = 91,552.\n\nSo total is 805,337 - 02,233 = ?\n\n814,844 minus 02,433:\n\n791,291 - 04,185 = 921,189\n\n109,611 - 3,603 = 681,669. \n\nSame result! So that confirms it. Therefore, the product is indeed 953,479. Okay, so the initial calculation was correct. I think that's solid.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the product of 356 and 952?.\n# Reasoning:.\nLet's think step by step. 952 has three digits, so that we can reason in three steps.  .\n1. Multiply 356 by the ones place digit 2 of 952:  .\n   \\(356 \\times 2 = 712\\).  .\n2. Multiply 356 by the tens place digit 50 of 952:  .\n   \\(356 \\times 50 = 17,\\!800\\).  .\n3. Multiply 356 by the hundreds place digit 900 of 952:  .\n   \\(356 \\times 900 = 320,\\!400\\).  .\nAnswer:  .\nNow, sum all the step results:  .\n\\(712 + 17,\\!800 + 320,\\!400 = 338,\\!912\\). Therefore, the answer is"]] 45%|####4     | 224/500 [1:09:09<1:30:02, 19.57s/it]
