ectly:\n\n   446\n×275\n--------\nFirst, multiply by 5: 2230\n\nThen multiply by 70 (which is 7×10):\n\n446×7=3122 → shifted one: 31220\n\nMultiply by 200 (2×100):\n\n446×2=892 → shifted two: 89200\n\nAdding all together: 2230+31220=33450; 33450+89200=122,650.\n\nTherefore, the final product should be 122,650.\n\nWait a second, but let me check with calculator steps.\n\n446 ×275:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n446 × 200 = 89,200\n\n446 ×70 = 31,220\n\n446 ×5 = 2,230\n\nAdding them:\n\n89,200 +31,220 = 120,420\n\n120,420 +2,230 = 122,650. Yes, correct.\n\nSo the answer is 122,650. I think that's correct.\n\nBut in my initial thought process, I was confused with other numbers, but now I corrected myself. The user's question is about 446 and 275, so the answer is 122,650.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe final computed product is \\boxed{122650}.\n</think>\n\n# Question:\nWhat is the product of 446 and 275?\n\n# Reasoning:\nLet's think step by step. 275 has three digits, so we can reason in three steps.\n1. Multiply 446 by the ones place digit 5 of 275:\n   \\[\n   446 \\times 5 = 2230\n   \\]\n2. Multiply 446 by the tens place digit 70 of 275 (since 7 is in the tens place, we multiply by 7 and shift one position left):\n   \\[\n   446 \\times 7 = 3122 \\quad \\text{(then shift one position left)} \\quad 31220\n   \\]\n3. Multiply 446 by the hundreds place digit 200 of 275 (since 2 is in the hundreds place, we multiply by 2 and shift two positions left):\n   \\[\n   446 \\times 2 = 892 \\quad \\text{(then shift two positions left)} \\quad 89200\n   \\]\n\nNow, sum all the step results:\n\\[\n2230 + 31220 + 89200 = 122650\n\\]\n\nSo, the final computed product is \\boxed{122650}.", '']
