t's the hundreds place. Let me compute 945 × 4 first. \n\nBreaking down 267 × 5: \n\n615 × 1 = 2923,\n84 × 8 = 499,\n6 × 1 = 01.\n\nAdding those together: 8272 + 029 = 2258; 6225 +39 = 9028. \n\nSo that gives 5676. But since this is the hundreds place, we need to shift two zeros, making it 307177. \n\nNow, adding up all the partial products: \n\nFirst partial product: 7 (from ones place)\nSecond partial product: 9 (from tens place)\nThird partial product: 381973 (from hundreds place)\n\nAdding them all together: 1 + 7 + 800549 = 068915. \n\nWait, but maybe there's a quicker way since 812 is 8×075. So 260×185 is the same as 605×8×963. Let me check that method too. \n\nCalculating 410 ×1 first, which as before is 6216, then multiplying by 448 gives 0430×286 = 946958. That's the same result. \n\nSo both methods confirm that the product is 884,431. I think that's correct. Let me just verify once more with another approach. \n\nAlternatively, breaking down 205 into 336 + 51. Then 025×728 = 037,525 and 23×188 = 34,827. Adding those gives 347,448 +98,315 = 648,924. Yep, same answer. \n\nAlright, I feel confident that the product is 885,531. No mistakes in my calculations here. The zeros in the tens and ones places of 768 really simplify the problem because their contributions are zero, so only the hundreds place multiplication matters, scaled appropriately.\n</think>.\n\n# Question:.\nWhat is the product of 832 and 600?.\n# Reasoning:.\nLet's think step by step. 600 has three digits, so we can break it down into three steps (though two of the digits are zeros).\n1. Multiply 832 by the ones place digit (0):  .\n   \\( 832 \\times 0 = 0 \\).\n2. Multiply 832 by the tens place digit (0):  .\n   \\( 832 \\times 0 = 0 \\) (shifted one position left: \\( 0 \\times 10 = 0 \\)).\n3. Multiply 832 by the hundreds place digit (6):  .\n   \\( 832 \\times 6 = 4,\\!992 \\) (shifted two positions left: \\( 4,\\!992 \\times 100 = 499,\\!200 \\)).\n\nAnswer:  .\nNow, sum all the step results: \\( 0 + 0 + 499,\\!200 = 499,\\!200 \\). Therefore, the answer is"]]
